 "result": "Both classes are in `src/tessel/views.py`.\n\n- **`View`** (line 16) is the base class. Its classmethod `to_view_func(name, ...)` builds the plain view function. The docstring example calls it as `CounterAPI.to_view_func(\"counter\")`.\n  - The inner `view(**kwargs)` (lines 106 and 115) creates an instance of the class, unless `init_every_request = False`, and calls `self.run_view(**kwargs)` through `active_app.ensure_sync`.\n  - It applies any `decorators`, then attaches `view_class`, `methods` and `provide_automatic_options` to the function (lines 129-134). The router uses `methods` to decide which HTTP methods the route accepts.\n- **`VerbView(View)`** (line 138) adds the per-method routing.\n  - `__init_subclass__` (line 165) runs when a subclass is defined. It scans for attributes named after HTTP methods (`http_method_funcs`) and sets `cls.methods` to the uppercase names, so defining `get` and `post` gives `{\"GET\", \"POST\"}`.\n  - `run_view` (line 182) does the dispatch. It looks up `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`, falls back to `get` for HEAD when there is no `head` handler, and calls the handler with the URL kwargs.\n\nThe result is registered with `app.attach_route_rule(url, view_func=...)`.",
